Objective
Exploit a blind SQL Injection vulnerability by using database errors as a TRUE/FALSE indicator to extract the administrator password.

Tools Used

Burp Suite Proxy
Burp Suite Intruder
Firefox
PortSwigger Web Security Academy


Vulnerability
The application was vulnerable to Blind SQL Injection through the TrackingId cookie.
Instead of displaying query results, the application returned an HTTP 500 Internal Server Error whenever a crafted SQL condition evaluated to TRUE.
This behavior created an error-based side channel that allowed sensitive information to be extracted.

Methodology

Intercepted the request in Burp Suite.
Sent the request to Intruder.
Injected a conditional SQL payload into the TrackingId cookie.
Used Intruder with a payload list containing letters (a–z) and numbers (0–9).
Observed HTTP 500 responses to identify the correct character for each password position.
Repeated the process for all 20 positions.
Logged in as the administrator using the recovered password.
Successfully solved the lab.


Key Lessons Learned

Blind SQL Injection does not require visible database output.
Error messages can act as a reliable TRUE/FALSE indicator.
Burp Intruder greatly simplifies repetitive character extraction.
Small differences in application behavior can reveal highly sensitive information.


Personal Reflection
Today's lab reinforced an important lesson: security isn't only about hiding information. Applications must also avoid leaking clues through different responses or error conditions.
Every small behavior matters.
